---
import { cta } from '../../lib/site';

// Screen tour of the actual build (public/demo.mp4, H.264 + AAC).
//
// WALKTHROUGH v2: the silent, caption-burned cut now follows ONE order —
// the Alvarez–Whitman wedding in the Maple & Main demo world — through the
// ACTUAL email send (the Mailpit capture inbox is on camera), the imperfect
// delivery, the inventory post, and the quoted-vs-today result. Captions are
// burned into the frames by the capture step; there is no narration track.
//
// FOUR THINGS HERE MOVE WITH THE FOOTAGE, EVERY TIME: the chip's duration, the
// sr-only transcript, the video's aria-label, and the hero link's duration
// (guard-pinned in check-landing-claims.mjs). They have drifted twice —
// commit 3e8d136 left the chip reading 48 sec over a 39.5s cut, and an
// earlier cut narrated a different menu than the order it quoted.
// Regenerate (app repo first: `npm run demo:seed` + `npm run demo:capture`,
// Mailpit up, preview on :4181 — see the app repo's demo/README.md):
//   APP=http://localhost:4181 node scripts/demo-video/capture-silent.mjs
//   node scripts/demo-video/assemble-silent.mjs   (ffmpeg-static + playwright-core, npm i --no-save)
---

	<section id="demo" class="container-page py-section-tight">
		<div data-reveal>
			<p class="eyebrow">The product, not a mockup</p>
			<h2 class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-h2 font-semibold">Watch one order go from recipe to sent purchase orders to the shelf.</h2>
			<p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lede text-ink-soft">
				A 180-guest wedding, start to finish: the costed recipe, the order&rsquo;s money,
				the purchase orders actually emailed to vendors, a delivery that isn&rsquo;t
				perfect, and the shelf that agrees at the end. Real interface, real sends into a
				capture inbox, every number labeled for what it means.
		</p>
		<p id="demo-transcript" class="sr-only">
				A silent, captioned screen recording of CostCook following one order start to
				finish. There is no narration; a music bed plays and every claim is written on
				screen, so nothing is carried by audio alone. A year of orders on the books, the
				next big one a wedding of 180 plates three days out. The braised short rib recipe
				with its costed lines, the beef carrying 93 percent of the plate. The order:
				180 guests at $68 a head is $12,240.00 of revenue against $4,845.61 of food,
				39.6 percent food cost, with the price that meets the 30 percent target named
				on screen. The same order as a shopping list grouped by vendor, in the whole
				packs you actually buy, Baldor totalling $690.82. A capture inbox showing the
				purchase orders the confirmation actually emailed, one per vendor, each with its
				own PO number; then one opened: PO-00783-002 to Green Valley Produce, five
				items, a $516.08 subtotal, payment terms and dock notes. Receiving: arugula a
				case short, recorded and saved for follow-up; baby spinach with an extra case,
				sixteen delivered where fifteen were quoted, $477.16 paid against the $410.40
				estimate. The ingredient&rsquo;s live cost panel showing the paid price as
				current cost with its source recorded. The inventory screen with the shelf counted
				today. Finally the order&rsquo;s comparison band: quoted at $26.92 a guest, $27.12
				at today&rsquo;s prices, a 0.7 percent move with the quote still holding.
			</p>

		<!-- Not styled as a ticket: real product footage should read as real,
		     not as another branded artifact. Poster is the vendor-grouped shop
		     list with its caption bar: packs, prices, and vendor subtotals all
		     legible in one frame. -->
		<!-- Centred and capped at the capture width: the footage is 1600x1000, and
		     stretching it past that only blurs it. -->
		<div class="relative mt-8 mx-auto max-w-[1600px]">
			<video
				controls
				playsinline
				preload="none"
				poster="/demo-poster.jpg"
				width="1600"
				height="1000"
				class="w-full border border-hairline bg-paper shadow-ticket"
				aria-label="Silent, captioned screen recording: CostCook prices a 180-guest wedding, emails its purchase orders, receives an imperfect delivery, and posts it to inventory"
				aria-describedby="demo-transcript"
			>
				<source src="/demo.webm" type="video/webm" />
				<source src="/demo.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
				<p>
					Your browser can&rsquo;t play this video.
					<a href="/demo.mp4" class="btn-quiet">Download it instead</a>
				</p>
			</video>
			<!-- Play affordance: the poster alone reads as a static screenshot.
			     Dark chip echoes the footage's own caption chips. Decorative
			     (the video carries the labels), lets taps fall through to the
			     video, removed on first play; shown only when scripting can
			     remove it, so no-JS playback is never covered. -->
			<span
				id="demo-play-badge"
				class="pointer-events-none absolute top-3 left-3 hidden items-center gap-2 rounded-full bg-ink/85 px-3.5 py-1.5 text-sm font-medium text-offwhite shadow-ticket [@media(scripting:enabled)]:inline-flex"
				aria-hidden="true"
			>
				<svg width="10" height="12" viewBox="0 0 10 12" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true">
					<path d="M0 0l10 6-10 6z"></path>
				</svg>
				Watch · 1 min 51 sec
			</span>
		</div>

		<a
			href={cta.href}
			target={cta.target}
			rel={cta.rel}
			aria-label={cta.ariaLabel}
			class="btn-quiet mt-8"
		>
			{cta.label}
		</a>
	</div>
</section>

<script>
	// One job: clear the play chip once the footage is rolling.
	const video = document.querySelector<HTMLVideoElement>('#demo video');
	const badge = document.getElementById('demo-play-badge');
	if (video && badge) {
		video.addEventListener('play', () => badge.remove(), { once: true });
	}
</script>
